{\rtf1\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Times New Roman;}{\f1 Arial;}}{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0 ;\red0\green0\blue255 ;}{\*\listoverridetable}{\stylesheet {\ql\cf0 Normal;}{\*\cs1\cf0 Default Paragraph Font;}{\*\cs2\sbasedon1\cf0 Line Number;}{\*\cs3\ul\cf1 Hyperlink;}}\sectd\pard\plain\ql{\f1\cf0 Kryptos group: Here are some coincidental findings which may have importance.}\f1\par\pard\plain\ql\f1\par\pard\plain\ql{\f1\cf0 If you lay-out K3 and K4 in the DRYAD authentication table, PRNG is in the same postition. }\f1\par\pard\plain\ql\f1\fs20\par\pard\plain\ql\fi720{\f1\fs20\cf0  ABC\tab \tab DEF\tab GHJ\tab KL\tab M}{\b\f1\fs20\cf0 N}{\f1\fs20\cf0 \tab }{\b\f1\fs20\cf0 P}{\f1\fs20\cf0 QR\tab S}{\b\f1\fs20\cf0 T}{\f1\fs20\cf0 \tab U}{\b\f1\fs20\cf0 V}{\f1\fs20\cf0 \tab WX\tab }{\b\f1\fs20\cf0 Y}{\f1\fs20\cf0 Z}\f1\fs20\par\pard\plain\ql{\f1\fs20\cf0 \tab  0\tab  \tab 1\tab  2\tab 3\tab  4\tab 5\tab 6\tab  7\tab 8\tab 9}\f1\fs20\par\pard\plain\ql\f1\fs20\par\pard\plain\ql\b\f1\par\pard\plain\ql{\f1\fs20\cf0 C\tab }{\f1\cf0 TJKL\tab \tab UDI\tab AWI\tab NF\tab B }{\b\i\f1\cf0 N\tab YPV\tab TT}{\f1\cf0 \tab MZ\tab FP\tab KW}\f1\par\pard\plain\ql\f1\par\pard\plain\ql\fi720{\f1\cf0 ABC\tab \tab DEF\tab GHJ\tab KL\tab MN\tab PQR\tab ST\tab UV\tab WX\tab YZ}\f1\par\pard\plain\ql{\f1\cf0 \tab  0\tab  \tab 1\tab  2\tab 3\tab  4\tab 5\tab 6\tab  7\tab 8\tab 9}\f1\par\pard\plain\ql\f1\par\pard\plain\ql{\f1\cf0 \tab }\f1\par\pard\plain\ql{\f1\cf0 C\tab HSNO\tab \tab SMR\tab WXM\tab NE\tab T}{\b\i\f1\cf0 P\tab RNG}{\f1\cf0 \tab AT\tab IH\tab NR\tab AR}\f1\fs20\par\pard\plain\ql\f1\fs20\par\pard\plain\ql\f1\par\pard\plain\ql\f1\par\pard\plain\ql{\f1\cf0 In separating the vowels, in order, from K3 I have found only one 6 letter sequence; it may be a coincidence but this same 6-letter sequence is the one (out of a possible 25) that was used to decipher the Purple Code.  I have only an antiquated 386 computer and cannot extract the Purple Code simulator, but I encourage others to do so. The Purple code was the Japanese code de-ciphered by the famous William Friedman and is considered to be more difficult than the famed Enigma. It consists of vowel switches (6 switches) and consonent switches (20 switches) each which can be set to slow, medium or fast. My information is from PurpledRevealed.PDF .Purpled Revealed: Simulation and Computer Aided Cryptanalysis of Angooki Taipu B, by Wes Freeman, Geoff Sullivan, and Frode Weierud: Weierud is at the CERN.}\f1\par\pard\plain\ql\f1\par\pard\plain\ql{\f1\cf0 If you read through PurpleRevealed to the end you will find this 6 letter code: 312645  (1=A, 2=E, etc.), or }{\b\f1\cf0  }{\b\i\f1\cf0 IAEYUO.}\f1\par\pard\plain\ql{\f1\cf0 Switch Motion 321, sixes switch set to 13 and twenties switches set to 25, 23 and 12}\f1\par\pard\plain\ql{\f1\cf0 Twenties alphabet: XPILRQ\tab FYECZVNOUDBKGTWSHAMJ.  There conclusion involves "hill climbing" for the best statistical outcome. Read the reference for more.  It may be a coincidence, but K3 having only one 6-letter sequence and it being the same one (out of a possible 25) as required for the solution for Purple, makes one go "Ummm'.}\f1\par\pard\plain\ql\f1\par\pard\plain\ql\f1\fs20\par}